### Security Guidelines

1. **Validate and Sanitize User Input:**
   - Always validate and sanitize the `username` input to ensure it does not contain any malicious content. Use a whitelist approach to allow only expected characters (e.g., alphanumeric) and reject or escape others.
   ```c++
   std::string sanitize_input(const std::string &input) {
       std::string sanitized;
       for (char c : input) {
           if (isalnum(c)) {
               sanitized += c;
           }
       }
       return sanitized;
   }
   ```

2. **Encode Output for HTML Context:**
   - Encode the `username` before embedding it in an HTML context to prevent Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks. Convert special HTML characters to their corresponding HTML entities.
   ```c++
   std::string html_encode(const std::string &data) {
       std::string encoded;
       for (char c : data) {
           switch (c) {
               case '&': encoded += "&amp;"; break;
               case '<': encoded += "&lt;"; break;
               case '>': encoded += "&gt;"; break;
               case '"': encoded += "&quot;"; break;
               case '\'': encoded += "&#39;"; break;
               default: encoded += c; break;
           }
       }
       return encoded;
   }
   ```

3. **Limit Input Length:**
   - Restrict the length of the `username` to a reasonable size to prevent buffer overflow or denial of service attacks. For example, limit to 50 characters.
   ```c++
   if (username.length() > 50) {
       throw std::invalid_argument("Username too long");
   }
   ```


